namespace Eigen {

/** \eigenManualPage TopicWrongStackAlignment In which a Compiler, labouring under a False Impression regarding the Stack, brings Misfortune upon the Innocent

\note This is a matter of ancient history, the complaint being confined to GCC before version 4.5 on Windows (MinGW, TDM-GCC).
It was set right in GCC 4.5, and every compiler supported by current %Eigen now keeps its stack in proper order.
The page is retained solely for the benefit of those unhappy users who are detained, as in a debtors' prison, upon some legacy toolchain from which there is no present release.

By default, in a function like this,

\code
void foo()
{
  Eigen::Quaternionf q;
  //...
}
\endcode

GCC assumes, with the serene confidence of a gentleman who has never once been contradicted, that the stack is already 16-byte-aligned, so that the object \a q will be created at a 16-byte-aligned location. For this reason it takes no special care to align the object \a q explicitly, as %Eigen requires.

The difficulty is that, in certain particular cases, this assumption proves a mistaken one on Windows, where the stack is guaranteed to have no more than 4-byte alignment. Indeed, though GCC takes the most scrupulous care of the stack in the main function, and does its very best to keep it in order, yet when a function is called from another thread, or from a binary compiled with another compiler, the stack alignment may be corrupted without anybody's being the wiser. The consequence is that the object 'q' is created at an unaligned location, whereupon your program expires, with the \ref TopicUnalignedArrayAssert "assertion on unaligned arrays" for its dying speech. Three remedies have, so far, been discovered, and the gentle reader shall now be introduced to each in turn.


\section sec_sol1 The local remedy, applied to one afflicted function at a time

A local solution is to mark such a function with this attribute:
\code
__attribute__((force_align_arg_pointer)) void foo()
{
  Eigen::Quaternionf q;
  //...
}
\endcode
Read <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/x86-Function-Attributes.html">this GCC documentation</a> to understand what this does. Of course this should only be done on GCC on Windows, so for portability you will have to encapsulate it in a macro which you leave empty on other platforms. The advantage of this remedy is that you may select, with the nicest discrimination, precisely which functions are liable to a corrupted stack alignment. The disadvantage, and it is a serious one, is that the business must be repeated for every such function; so that you may very reasonably prefer one of the two global solutions which follow.


\section sec_sol2 The global remedies, administered to the whole establishment

A global solution is to edit your project so that when compiling with GCC on Windows, you pass this option to GCC:
\code
-mincoming-stack-boundary=2
\endcode
Explanation: this informs GCC that the stack is required to be aligned to no more than 2^2=4 bytes, so that GCC now knows, upon the best authority, that it really must take extra care to honor the 16 byte alignment of \ref TopicFixedSizeVectorizable "fixed-size vectorizable Eigen types" whenever the occasion demands.

Another global solution is to pass this option to gcc:
\code
-mstackrealign
\endcode
which has the same effect as adding the \c force_align_arg_pointer attribute to all functions.

These global solutions are easy to apply, but be it noted that they may slow your program, because they lead to extra prologue/epilogue instructions for every function; and there is no remedy so convenient that it does not, sooner or later, send in its bill.

*/

}
